  "text": " Qualcomm Cloud AI 100 Ultra – 150W AI Accelerator Card with 128GB at 548GB/s | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Qualcomm Cloud AI 100 Ultra – 150W AI Accelerator Card with 128GB at 548GB/s ( qualcomm.com ) 117 points by peter_d_sherman 8 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 48 comments help \n \n tokepi 7 hours ago | next [–] \n If the specs seem outdated, it’s because this chip launched in 2024. It’s available at cloud providers including Cirrascale reply rpcope1 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Given the rush to dig MI50s and V100s out of the trash and press them into service, 2024 seems like future space alien technology. reply jauntywundrkind 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Thats relieving! I'm starting to believe in Qualcomm! The Snapdragon X2 Elite Extreme is alas only available in a single system, with it's tri-channel on-package (!) memory good for 228 GB/s. But it's a beast. The core is just good, even in the mainstream form in other systems: some of the best single core performance you can get, shy of Apple. And they are upstreaming their stuff much better! They hired a bunch of really good known FOSS developers and it seems to be working! Now they are talking up High Bandwidth Compute as their next big thing. And I want to believe, even if it makes me nervous that they too intend to leave consumer computing completely in the dust. https://www.qualcomm.com/news/onq/2026/11/qualcomm-hbc-valid... This is, as commented, at M4 Max levels of GB/s. It is not impressive. It has some uses! But if this was their modern opening salvo I would not be impressed. reply redanddead 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > I'm starting to believe in Qualcomm Someone drop the YouTube link of their robot presentation reply gleenn 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The way it drops is priceless. https://youtube.com/shorts/aZmt5E151Bk reply 3eb7988a1663 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Those guys were fast on the draw to cover the body with those sheets. reply thway15269037 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n What's the price? reply simlevesque 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n According to their own website, they cost 1 cent. reply wewewedxfgdf 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Less than I expected for this level of GPU power. It's a trend I like. reply bigwheels 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n 8,750 in stock at 1 cent, too. The more expensive 47 and 63 cent variants are all sold out. reply thway15269037 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yeah, I clicked that too. But knowing qualcomm they obviously cost an arm and a leg. Still interesting how much. reply bigwheels 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n My research indicates the price is $10-17K USD. reply InsidiousWombat 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n that tracks, $0.01 for chip, and $9999.9 for the memory reply IgorPartola 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I mean at ten bucks it’s not a bad deal but that’s quite a range. reply dingdingdang 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Honestly, let me buy one, it's many times faster than the minipc I run local ai on so if the price is right I'm in. reply martin1975 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n hopefully less than 5090? reply wmf 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Not a chance. reply pearl_panel5 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Curious how the memory bandwidth actually holds up under sustained inference loads compared to the spec sheet numbers. reply skavi 48 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n is this hexagon? reply rsendv 4 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n Yeah, bunch of HTPs reply swiftcoder 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n So it's an M4 Max Mac Studio, RAM-wise, with a GPU's worth of compute? reply trvz 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n (I know it's you comparing it to the M4 Max, not them, but:) it's Qualcomm, so it'll be compared to the M4 Max, while being slower than a M2 Max. reply zitterbewegung 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Sort of interesting design It has half a terabyte of SRAM on the accelerator with a 128 GB of RAM replacing HBM with SRAM but keeping regular RAM. reply 2001zhaozhao 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n half a gigabyte not terabyte lol reply zrm 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yeah, this thing is unusual. It resembles a consumer GPU but using normal DRAM with more channels, so then you get more RAM but less memory bandwidth than GDDR/HBM, which is the same thing Apple does. And the TDP is pretty attractive for what it is. But what is that amount of SRAM for? It simultaneously seems like a lot, because SRAM is so expensive, but still not very much, because what can you fit in 0.5GB? Is there a specific workload that needs something that looks like this? reply zamadatix 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The Intel D270, Nvidia DGX Spark, and (somewhat) AMD Strix Halo also use the same approach, so it seems to be getting pretty common. SRAM wise it doesn't seem too insane. About the same ratio of memory:SRAM as the 5090 (IIRC). Still more than I would have expected. I assume they found it helps process the current layer faster or something. reply 0917236141 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Seems like a reasonable general-purpose card. reply aetherspawn 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Ok so it’s significantly worse than Apple M6 and we don’t know if it’s cheaper or not Edit: Ah sorry you’re right, the bandwidth is lower but the TOPS is a lot higher. I’m not sure how much effect you get from higher TOPS with such low bandwidth though. reply zozbot234 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > I’m not sure how much effect you get from higher TOPS with such low bandwidth though. Raised arithmetic intensity. It would be quite good for LLM prefill, and decode would want to adapt by pushing parallelism higher (saving on memory bandwidth by reusing the same layer weights for multiple streams) or, if unable to do so (e.g. due to KV cache constraints) by resorting to speculative decoding (where the expensive 'validate' step handles tokens in parallel within a single stream). It does not make the platform inherently unviable. It would also do well at other tasks such as image/video generation, which is already quite compute bound. reply dist-epoch 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Apple M6 has 80-100 TOPS. Qualcomm Cloud AI 100 Ultra has 800 TOPS, almost 10 times more. reply grosswait 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This could be very fast at running embedding models (if it supports the right modes) reply ipsum2 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Considering that memory bandwidth is the most important for inference for most neural networks, that matters more. reply dist-epoch 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Modern inference stacks use different GPUs for decode versus prefill. This is a GPU suitable for prefill. reply varispeed 7 hours ago | prev [–] \n Aren't these specs already outdated? Seems meh. I would think players would like to bring something new to the table instead of bringing what was okay two years ago or so. Give me 1TB RAM and 5TB/s or something that exciting. reply entrope 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n For most purposes, this seems reasonable? This looks like a single-height card, so fitting four of these in a workstation or server would give 512 GB RAM and 2 TB/s total bandwidth. That's pretty attractive for a lot of current LLMs, even if one wishes for 1 TB capacity at 5 TB/s bandwidth. reply throwawayffffas 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If you are going down this path, MI350P and RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell is what you would want. A lot more compute a lot more vram bandwidth. reply entrope 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Maybe. I don't know power for the MI350, but the RTX PRO 6000 is normally a 200-300W card, so you pay 2x the power for 3/4th the RAM and 3x the bandwidth. The performance frontier looks very different in terms of power to hold a mid-size model and throughout per user or across users. Price and inference stack would be big factors in what I would choose. reply throwawayffffas 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n MI350P, H200 NVL and Nvidia RTX PRO 6000 are all 600W but compute wise all of them should be 3 to 4 times more performant than A100 Ultra according to their specifications. https://www.servethehome.com/the-amd-instinct-mi350p-is-a-hb... reply zozbot234 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > A lot more compute a lot more vram bandwidth. But this gives you significantly more memory capacity for a compute/vram bandwidth tradeoff (i.e. arithmetic intensity) that's ultimately comparable to Blackwell, perhaps even more skewed towards compute. This seems good if you care about being able to store larger sparse models and more KV caches because that ultimately bottlenecks your ability to do inference. reply varispeed 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n But there are already plenty of products for most purposes. reply tempest_ 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n 5 year old GPUs are still at crazy prices. Plenty of businesses want in local inference and are priced out. reply varispeed 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You can't do quality inference on such a toy. It's a myth. reply redanddead 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n A great opportunity for huawei Nobody helps China like American corps helps China. Domestic anticompetitive behavior spurs demand for international competition reply throwawayffffas 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This is all the CPU companies playing catch up with Nvidia and AMD. The thing you are describing is much closer to AMD and Nvidia latest generation server offerings. See MI455X for the absolute cutting edge and MI350P for something you can use outside of a datacenter. reply mciancia 7 hours ago | parent | prev [–] \n 8 of those cards give you 1TB of ram and 4.4TB/s reply PhunkyPhil 7 hours ago | root | parent [–] \n It may be a semantic thing but I don't think bandwidth pooling works like that? Unless your blocks are distributed perfectly between cards there's going to be data passed between each card and from VRAM to compute. Correct me if I'm wrong though reply msandford 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think the way it works is that the weights have to get streamed through the compute and the weights can be split very easily. With multiple cards you do have a coordination/communication phase after each layer of weights but that's usually much less significant than the compute phase. reply throwawayffffas 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev [–] \n It depends on how you slice the weights. You can split along layers which means each GPU waits for the previous one to finish or you can split the tensors across gpus. Splitting the tensors is closer to n times the effective vram bandwidth but there are still overhead, sync waits, and reduce steps that eat away at the overall performance. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
