 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50366999": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T04:25:13.637Z",
  "bytes": 40654,
  "text": " Kimi K3.1 Pro model weights are out | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Kimi K3.1 Pro model weights are out ( huggingface.co ) 422 points by abrarthought 12 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 124 comments help \n \n __jf__ 12 hours ago | next [–] \n Subtle quote from their website: > Advancing the Frontier of Open Intelligence. Too bad you probably need something like an 8x B300 node to run this. Black Friday is coming soon though, but I fear this open intelligence will be pacing anyone's liquidity frontier so I'll probably keep trailing the frontier for a while with nvidia/qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4 on dual RTX PRO 4000 in TP=2. I can almost fit 450k context in there. reply bityard 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Models this size aren't targeting hobbyists running models in their bedrooms on gaming/surplus hardware bought with disposable income. This model is for researchers who already have racks of equipment, inference providers who sell access to models, and for businesses who must (or choose to) use on-prem models. All of which have to pay to use the model if their revenue rises beyond certain thresholds: https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3.1-Pro/blob/main/LI... reply limagnolia 21 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Or, you can just not pay and save that money to defend yourself in case they do sue you. Because it isn't established that model weights are copyrightable. reply zozbot234 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's only \"model as a service\" providers beyond the stated threshold that have to pay. No issues for any kind of purely internal use. reply SR2Z 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Considering the likelihood of Moonshot being able to collect a judgement for violating these terms in an American court is basically zero, and the relative ease of finding the weights without the licensing agreement, \"have to pay\" seems very generous. reply largbae 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Most enterprises capable of running that much gear won't take that risk even if it is unenforceable. Oracle has made a great living threatening audits of companies who download its updates. reply SR2Z 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yeah, but only Western companies who might have to defend themselves in court. I have no doubt that pirated copies of Oracle DB and the JDK are frequently used in China. reply demo4567 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n abd why should we expect this to be the case? I can atleast buy old server hardware for cheap. GPUs are currently locked down the whole industry. reply minton 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > more than $20 million…in monthly revenue Sign me up! reply zozbot234 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The model-as-a-service clause (which is new to the K3 series) actually only requires $20 million over 12 months - i.e. yearly revenue. reply FuriouslyAdrift 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n We're running Kimi 2.8 on a 4x AMD MI300A single server on-premise that cost about $110k. We will break even from our old Claude spend in about 2 weeks. It services 30-40 analysts and staff concurrently and is faster than any of the SaaS out there for us. reply edg5000 6 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Very cool reply flossly 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Wow... I'd like to know more. 40 analysts not spending 100USD/month is 4k/month saved. That means you break even in 27 months. But you probably used Claude much more than that. Also what harness do you use? Any problems you encountered on the way? reply FuriouslyAdrift 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Just one of our analysts was burning around 30-40 million tokens per project. EDIT: per project, not per month EDIT 2: digging out an old bill from 8 months ago when we bought the server, $14k per month is what we were running and it was going up, a lot. reply Moto74