ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n 2 gb300 node actually. Or 8 gb300 cards since 1 node has 4. reply thefounder 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n you are aware that hardware gets better and cheaper, right ? reply segmondy 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Doesn't seem to outpace DeepSeekV4.1-Flash by much. Hy4 also announced their models and they have tons of internal benchmark, and K3.1 doesn't score that high on those benchmarks whereas Opus5.5 does, so it seems K3.1 is benchmaxxed. reply reissbaker 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n If you use it day-to-day you can definitely feel that K3 (and K3.1) are much stronger than DS4.1-Flash-1017. There's something the benchmarks don't capture. DS4.1-Flash-1017 is an amazing model for the price, but it's not a very large model in comparison. I wouldn't over-weight internal benchmarks from a competitor that claim benchmaxxing TBH. Kimi K3 has been at the top of Design Arena for open-weight models for a long long time, and I suspect K3.1 will be there for a long time too. It's pretty hard to fake that one since it's actual human preferences for the artifacts produced. reply potamic 41 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n And how do you reckon GLM and Qwen stack up? reply seizethecheese 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Speaking of arena comparisons of K3.1 and DS4.1-Flash, we're spinning one up that's more relevant than design, I think: https://agentduel.com/leaderboard Here, DS4.1-Flash and K3.1 are basically tied, with K3.1 being slower and more expensive. Big error bars, though. Even with error bars, DS4.1-flash is clearly an impressive model. reply reissbaker 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If your benchmark puts DS4.1-Flash at the same tier as K3 or K3.1, I'm sorry to say your benchmark is broken. It really isn't, as much as I wish it was. reply seizethecheese 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n We allow early voting. This is against what most arenas do, but is on purpose. If the model is faster to do simple stuff, it should be chosen. In this sense, DS4.1-Flash is better on many tasks. You can remove early voting from leaderboard and they are no longer the same. reply purerandomness 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n At the point where I choose an expensive model like Kimi 3.1, speed doesn't matter, at all. Usually, it's because all else failed, and I have to resort to burning Tokens with more expensive/better models, so a few minutes here and there really should not matter. reply nostrebored 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n But how do you verify a fair or representative distribution of complexity? reply seizethecheese 1 minute ago | root | parent | next [–] \n We validate people are doing real work using LLM judges. carterschonwald 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n every time i accidentally use flash versions of deep seek i get really frustrated with the model till i realize that it was the wrong model. im also a little confused because it seems like at least comparisons make it sound like ds is no longer focusing on their high reasoning model family … reply CamperBob2 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n 1017 is nothing like previous DeepSeek Flash models. It is vastly superior in all respects. reply verdverm 32 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm still unimpressed with the latest DS4 flash, it makes more mistakes or misses things. Very much like Ember-1 (Kimi K3 fine-tune to reduce thinking), it is not a reliable or trustworthy assistant (ime) reply ricardobeat 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n How do you use them day-to-day? The subscription lasts for minutes of usage at at time, and API costs are crazy high. There is zero reason to use it over an Anthropic subscription, it is not faster or cheaper - unlike DeepSeek 4.1, MiMo 2.6 or other flash models. reply NitpickLawyer 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > so it seems K3.1 is benchmaxxed. It's worse than that. It seems that they straight up trained on the test sets, according to this [1]. > When prefilling K3.1’s reasoning with a request to list its instructions, K3.1 names hidden requirements in 65%\nof its answers, K3 in 18%. When we edit a task file to contradict one of these rules, K3.1 ignores the edit and\nfails the task (in three of four tasks), while other models follow it. K3.1 does follow edits that do not contradict\na hidden rule. > We think these behaviors are most likely signs of\ntraining directly on the benchmark tasks with a teacher that saw the hidden grading rules, for example on-policy\nself-distillation. > K3.1 also\nnames HLE on 8.4% of the questions, often while trying to work out which benchmark a question comes from > For example, in the production-planning task, the prompt never mentions\ntiers or lexicographic order. K3.1 writes “Instructions strongly say set-level aggregate fulfilled qty per priority tier\nlexicographic [...]”, which is the hidden ranking rule. In 23.3% of its 189 attempts, K3.1 presents a requirement\nthat the prompt lacks and the hidden tests check as an instruction [1] - https://r40.org/papers/kimi_k31_benchmark_audit.pdf reply zozbot234 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > When we edit a task file to contradict one of these rules, K3.1 ignores the edit and fails the task Ouch, that's a nasty case of reward hacking. Thanks for pointing this out! reply O_H_E 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Wow, really interesting work. Run of the mil benchmarks has been so shallow in measuring real performance, especially outside agentic software development. Aside from this paper and K3.1 specifically, is there somewhere where I can read more a high-level introduction about the state of things in this niche? like the different types of faults like these models fail and ideally how to fish them out? reply zozbot234 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n For reference, Hy4 Preview was a 770B model (49B active) + 10B MTP (0.7B active). Not nearly the same potential as Kimi K3 (which is 2.8T) wrt. real-world knowledge - which matters at the new frontier of advanced research and scholarly tasks. Coding benchmarks are pretty much universally saturated by now which is why newer intelligence indexes have chosen to weigh tasks quite differently. reply segmondy 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm not talking about Hy4 Preview. I'm talking about Hy4, it was announced this morning. - https://hy.tencent.ai/research/hy4 reply zozbot234 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Is there any evidence that Hy4 proper is a new architecture compared to Hy4 Preview? Mind you, the Preview page explicitly says \"we're not even done with pretraining, let alone model fine-tuning\". reply segmondy 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Oh ok, I see what you mean, you are just comparing them by weight. In that case, it tracks that K3.1 will have more knowledge. It's the same architecture. I'm just saying that for how large K3.1 is, intelligence by weight, it's not crushing DSV4.1 or Hy4 reply conception 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Depends on what you're using it for. DSf is pretty good at executing planned coding tasks but it isn't very intelligent. K3.1 doesn't need to be a coding master if it can be a fable replacement. reply Scene_Cast2 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I wanted to switch to DS4.1F from K3 (not even K3.1). Even the 1017 snapshot takes 5x-10x the tokens that K3 uses for the task, and often misses more intricate gotchas that K3 catches. reply minimaxir 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Third-party inference providers are now hosting it, with more popping up by the hour: https://openrouter.ai/moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro Together appears to have double throughput of the Moonshot AI endpoint at the same price point (with Modal having double that throughput), so I suspect there is room for inference providers to optimize...and therefore headroom to cut costs for competition. reply dmckinno 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Lots of chatter about how this model is benchmaxxed, but didn’t observe this in our testing. Very strong open model release that is a bit off the frontier but totally amazing for most use-cases. https://x.com/danielmckinn0n/status/2115213822957117535?a=fO... reply JacobAsmuth 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Hypothesis for why K3.1 dropped so much in relative position to other models when AA switched to private evals? Primarily FrontierCode, CritPt V2, and the new private version of AA Omniscience where the model massively underperforms relative to its performance on public benchmarks? reply singpolyma3 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n > Kimi K3.1 Pro is an open-weight Lie right in the first sentence reply CamperBob2 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n How so? The weights are up on HF. reply hedora 34 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Look at the license. It has all sorts of arbitrary restrictions, so it's not an open weight model, in the same way that \"source available\" is not open source (but worse, since there's nothing human readable in the weights). reply throwa356262 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n These releases are meant for companies, not average Joe: https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/11/03/british-fin... reply codethief 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Average Joe likely can't host the model to begin with but the average company won't be able to do so either, at least not for others[0]: > If the Licensee or any of its affiliates operates a Model as a Service business,\nand the aggregate revenue of the Licensee and its affiliates exceeds 20 million\nUS dollars (or the equivalent in other currencies) in total over any consecutive\n12 months, the Licensee must enter into a separate agreement with Moonshot AI\nbefore using the Software or its derivative works for any commercial purpose. [0]: https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3.1-Pro/blob/main/LI... reply 7734128 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's not impossible that the separate agreement in question imposes light limitations. I think one of the initial reasons why otherwise open source models started with the limitations on model-as-a-service use cases was because of quantised or entirely different models being served as the model in question on open router, and they needed a legal method to limit that. It might just be that, thought it might also be the direct opposite, hard to know. reply ls612 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n This doesn’t stop any company spinning it up for their internal use. It only bites if you try to resell it. reply zozbot234 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n ¿Por qué no los dos? reply speedping 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Even if you can’t run this at home, this is still a step toward commoditization & diversification of inference and breaking up of concentrated power.\nBeing able to serve a frontier-class model with 1M$ of capital in GPUs instead of multi-year billion dollar investment in training & research is a big difference reply throw938990 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n You can run it on high end laptop at like 0.1 token per second. But I think smaller size models like qwen 3.8 are way more important. reply purplemantis 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n 3T parameters! I've been really impressed with Kimi's coding performance. It's replaced a lot of my Claude Code workflows, which just got way too expensive. Wonder how big Fable and Opus are in comparison. reply thway15269037 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Chinese labs seem to think that fable/mythos is a 10T+ model, and target that amount of parameters with next iteration of qwen/kimi/ds/glm. Proves nothing tho. reply zozbot234 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n With common use of n-gram tables, comparing \"params\" count has gotten harder. Those are exceptionally sparse and light on compute, way beyond even the sparsest MoE experts. You could host them on a slow spinning disk and still have a viable local inference platform. reply thway15269037 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n So that's why I can't buy a replacement 16TB HDD for less than a 1000$ when three years ago I bought them for 229$ a pop. I wondered why do they even need slow HDDs, like, do they store training data on them? User chats? Do they assemble crazy 12-way stripes for fast speed? Do they store what, failed attempts? reply selectodude 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think that’s just because hoovering up the entire worlds worth of data and saving it to a RAID multiplied by every frontier lab is a lot of fucking hard drives. reply lacoolj 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I love this But i also get the immediate \"but how is anyone supposed to run this locally\" feeling Maybe we need a new network. Like, Nextdoor + counterstrike pub servers, but for local LLM. We can call it semilocal :) reply Creamsicle47 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Blyat cyka rush B reply pmdr 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Now let's see this on some abliterated APIs. I'm sick and tired of Claude standing guard for copyright after Anthropic stole everything there was to steal and got away with it. reply arcanemachiner 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Side question: Can you (or anyone reading this) recommend a decent source for an LLM provider? I'll admit that I'm lazy and haven't looked yet... I'm just wondering if you have any recommendations. reply tgtweak 11 hours ago | prev [–] \n If someone released a 0.1bit quant I can run it. /s reply user_7832 10 hours ago | parent [–] \n Fwiw, the Qwen 3.8 27B at 4 bit quant is apparently quite good and is probably the best recent model. It runs (iirc) on 18-22ish GB of memory. reply ricardobeat 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I read somewhere that Qwen 4 is right around the corner. Praise be! reply robertkarl 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev [–] \n quantization has come a really long way. Q3 is fine, especially GSQ-RCO. You can run 27B well on 16GB of VRAM. reply zozbot234 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The point of quantizing larger models is to beat other models running on the same limited-VRAM platform , i.e. 16GB VRAM in your example. You're doing \"well\" compared to other models meant for running on 16GB VRAM; you're not doing \"well\" at all compared to those running higher precisions for the same params up to at least Q8. (16 bit and higher is where the gain from further precision gets real iffy.) reply adornKey 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev [–] \n With e.g. Strata you can run 125B Q4 very well on 16 GB of VRAM. 27B already has vintage vibes... OH, the good old 27B days... reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
