mentions proving a set of weights, but not how those are represented? What would stop a host using true weights for generating the signature, while running on quantized versions? > We prove an exact integer version of the published weights, and a proof asserts that each checked token is that model’s greedy choice. A reply from a floating-point implementation, or sampled decoding, could depart at some position... So, no...? reply l9o 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I think it would catch that, at least for open weights. From the repo docs [1] it looks like this type of attestation is even supported for open weight models: you commit with the public weights and publish the seed, so anyone can recompute the commitment from the Hugging Face files and check the provider is running the exact checkpoint and quantization they say they are. That's what they did with their Maverick demo. That said, it only works for greedy decoding and short contexts for now, and it's 70-95x the GPU time, so there may be cheaper ways to do this like TEEs. [1] https://github.com/anthropics/FlashProve/blob/main/docs/what... reply jrflo 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Maybe it's possible to tell by looking at the size of that error? > The integer version is generally expected and empirically tested to be within some small error rate of true floating-point inference generically (even taking into account non-determinism of floating-point computations), though this does not cover adversarially designed prompts. reply ajb 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Interesting, but I'm not sure the property it attests is one we care about that much. Or rather, it's one you'd expect Anthropic not to tell you it needs to prove. I mean it tells you that you're not being stiffed by Anthropic using a cheaper model and that they haven't literally been MiTMd, but that's kind of table stakes. Otherwise it's not telling you much useful. It's not telling you that your data hasn't been exfiltrated, and it's not telling you any useful property of the answer. reply velcrovan 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > I mean it tells you that you're not being stiffed by Anthropic using a cheaper model and that they haven't literally been MiTMd, but that's kind of table stakes. That's literally the most common gripe I see from Anthropic users and, so far from being \"table stakes\" there no existing mechanism by which those users could verify it is or isn't happening. If Anthropic can figure this out then most of this \"is it just me or is Opus hurr durr today\" noise can evaporate. reply grey-area 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n At first I thought this was work on the far more important problem of proving that LLM output is provable and correct given some source of truth. But it's about the trivial problem of 'proving' which model is being used. Who will find this useful? I can't really think of a use for it, because if you don't trust the provider of your models, it's game over really - you're already giving them all sorts of private data, you're relying on their service, you have to trust them to some extent and this doesn't really help because unless you are running the service yourself. Seems like more of an internal problem for Anthropic than something that would interest others. reply OneDeuxTriSeiGo 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n The main benefits are: 1. it proves your models are running correctly. FP ops tend to deviate in small amounts between hardware providers and tensor ops even more so due to the relatively new and unstable standardisation environment atm. So it proves your HW configuration is delivering outputs correctly. i.e. it's a good test for what is currently a very non-trivial validation environment. 2. it proves your external provider is running the inference they said they are. i.e. it eliminates the concerns of model providers running quantized versions silently behind the scenes since quantization breaks the proof and you need to use a new, updated proof signature for the quantized version. 3. likewise for fine tuned or tampered models. Or outright substitution for another model (ex the accusation by OAI and Anthropic that Kimi and DS and co are periodically forwarding requests to their platforms to harvest responses for knowledge distillation). All in all it's a nice chain of trust utility if it can evolve out of the toy stage and reach production viability (since rn it's limited to int-only weights and 1k context which is practically useless atm). reply manugo4 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Also useful to audit in case we pause the development of AI and we need to check inference is being done with known models. reply skybrian 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n For example, there are Chinese resellers that promise to provide access to Claude (which isn't officially available in China) and defraud users by substituting something else. They're obviously sketchy in all sorts of ways, but in theory, this would prevent one kind of fraud. It might also provide an additional guarantee for users of Open Router and Fireworks. reply grey-area 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n But if you’re using them they’re probably already stealing your data, fake-models are the least of your problems. reply nater5000 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n >Who will find this useful? It's worth reading past the opening paragraph before asking these kinds of questions: >When a user sends a request to an AI model, today they have no way of knowing for certain that the model they selected is the one that generated the response. For everyday uses, in which users mostly care about the output and not necessarily which specific model produced it, this is a less pressing concern. But there are several cases where such assurance would be useful. For example, independent evaluators who test AI models before and after release need to know that the model they are evaluating is the model being served. Clients who use a model through a third-party routing service want assurance that the tokens came from the model they paid for. And if a cyberattacker modifies the token stream while pretending to be a given model, a user has no way to detect it from the output alone. This is from the first paragraph in the Introduction. reply grey-area 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I didn't find any of it compelling, perhaps you do. That's what I was asking really, which of these use-cases is valuable for you? For me if you use a routing service you have to trust it, it sees all your inputs and outputs. If a cyberattacker can modify the token stream (i.e. man in the middle your traffic), you should be worried about a lot more than which model you're using! reply nater5000 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n >which of these use-cases is valuable for you? None of these are directly valuable to me. But you asked \"Who will find this useful?\" and I gave you the answer to that question (provided by Anthropic in this post). Not everything being worked on will be useful for you. It's just bizarre that you'd be trying to make these arguments. >If a cyberattacker can modify the token stream (i.e. man in the middle your traffic), you should be worried about a lot more than which model you're using! Sure, so it'd be great if you could know if someone is modifying your token stream. If only there was some advancement in provable inference that could allow you to verify that what you're getting from a provider is what you expect to get from them. reply grey-area 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If only the internet had some way to encrypt data in transit between two parties. This is a solved problem if you trust your counterparty and if you don’t it’s the least of your problems. All this provides is a tag saying 'x' version (which your counterparty defines anyway) is being used. reply hobofan 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n If one were to listen to /r/claude or /r/codex where half the posts are complaining about models being \"lobotomized\", one could think that this is the most valuable thing there is! In all seriousness, this is mostly just one of those defense in depth kind of things. Data exfiltration via a compromised provider is also a very different class of vulnerabilities than prompt injection via malicious tool calls. reply pxmpxm 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Customers care about model output being correct, Anthropic cares about model output being generated by Anthropic model. This solves for the latter with a bunch of handwaving for the former. The end goal is gonna be FUD for cheaper model routing because that obviously is at odds with their biz model. reply elzbardico 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You developed your agent implementation and more importantly, evaluated it, against a specific model. You don't want all you eval work to go to waste just because the discount inference provider you're using decided AcmeModel4.23 is as good as the Anthropics GilgameshEpic7.2 that you used to validate your solution. We already have trouble enough with model retirement. reply porridgeraisin 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Off the top of my head, it is useful when there are content-level proxies between the client and anthropic's API server, of which there are many these days. reply xpct 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > As models become capable of improving and deploying themselves ... how can a company be sure of which weights are actually running on its servers? I find this a bit odd and backwards. Why do we choose not to make the deployment code to be robust, but instead to observe the model inference as proven? This moves unobservability layer to the system that collects the proofs. Given the assumption that code is fluid and LLMs are rewriting it often, how can we know it isn't being abused against the observer? Goes without saying, it's also disappointing that this has nothing to do with making the model inference observable for consumers. reply Loquebantur 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Anthropic here is solving one of their own problems, how to make sure you don't combine other company's models with their stuff for example, and make it sound as if they did it for you. It makes the inference more expensive and helps controlling the users. reply fritzo 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think some model providers are implementing \"eyes glaze over\" where if you ask a boring simple question they route it to a cheap model but charge you for an expensive model, so consumers may want a proof of work certificate to know they're getting what they pay for. reply xpct 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think a consumer facing system would be fantastic, but from how they're framing it in the article, it's all about internal company monitoring, with some fearmongering around LLM observability. reply seurimas 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I assume this alludes to their prior work with models running on smart TVs being able to setup inference on additional smart TVs. The idea being that you can't be sure that, having gone through the whole deployment process, your hardware has not been subverted to run a different model. Any level of \"robust\" code you can imagine to prevent this in the traditional ways could be subverted on the fly by a capable model. > This moves unobservability layer to the system that collects the proofs. I'd say it's adding security through a secondary system, rather than displacing security to a secondary system. Security by layers. reply EliKarasik 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n We're working on provable inference at Attestable ( https://attestable.com/ ) - our solution is one of the comparisons provided. We've published initial performance metrics in August of 87 t/s on Muse Glimmer 30B (a dense model) - orders of magnitude faster than existing OS projects. Antropic's FlashProve supports only a fully integer model with a small context length (significantly hurting evals). There is significant progress since then, and we'll publish more results soon. reply thereader12 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It feels kinda yucky reading through a document that spends half the time saying how this great for our regulatory capture efforts and how they won't have to show anyone the wieghts. It's not even a bad thing, you want to know you are using what you think you are, but they are advertising the side effects as features. reply willy_k 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n In modern sv-wallstreet corporateness you need to be bashful over any attempt to do good for good’s sake. reply stevemk14ebr 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n This obviously an attempt at being able to say a response came from an anthropic model vs say some other model via a model router. As for me, I will keep using my model router, I don't care to be charged more for a name brand. I don't care about the brand at all, it's a commodity. reply dnnehgf 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n until your company gets sued for using Dangerous and Shoddy Foreign models and the boss says you can only use the Trustworthy and Premium Aligned labs' models. reply aetherspawn 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n So can we get proof that the inference was done on the model we actually paid-for and not some nerfed version. What’s the point of a SOTA model if, by 3 weeks later, it’s dumber than the previous generation at launch. (If you did this random substitution with physical goods it would probably be illegal or very frowned upon…) reply fscaramuzza 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n My PhD is on auditability of AI systems, and we are exactly working on the engineering of ZKP in this context. I can confirm through our industry partner (AI Assurance and auditing), that black box auditing is indeed a huge problem for them, and snarks have shown their potential in this context. It's great to see Anthropic confirming our early results. reply ngruhn 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I'm blind to the problem being solved here. Can you motivate it? Why do I need more than normal HTTPS on the token API? reply fscaramuzza 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n HTTPS makes sure you’re communicating on a secure channel with the API endpoint. ZKPs guarantees that the computation happened correctly behind the endpoint. The work from Anthropic isn't a new idea, but they made the process more efficient, as inference verification is one of the biggest bottlenecks in the AI lifecycle. Beyond that, there's a lot more that can be proven, like showing a model was trained on a specific dataset with certain properties. From an auditing perspective, this used to be handled either by showing the data (which is pretty rare), checking samples (which has been shown to be easily hackable), or similar workarounds. For inference, an auditor might want to interact with a model on demand and have absolute certainty that the model they're talking to is actually the one declared, and not a substitute. reply grey-area 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I’m not sure what you mean by correctly here - do you mean with the model expected? That’s not related to correctness. Pleased to hear somebody cares about this. What is it a bottleneck for though? Are people actually doing this right now in non-academic settings? I had the impression this is not in fact usable with larger models. reply ngruhn 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > guarantees that the computation happened correctly behind the endpoint but why do I care about that? can you some example \"user stories\"? reply brcmthrowaway 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n So, you got beaten to the punch? reply fscaramuzza 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Well luckily not really, their work is more foundational-y, the idea is not new, but they made it more efficient and practical. On our side, we work more on the integration of these protocol in production systems, but we are pretty much on the frontier and is not very common to see such big companies experimenting with this technology. Microsoft already tried something [1] but I'm not sure if it ever went further than a research prototype. [1] https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.02675 reply lrvick 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Very cool. Now prove you are unable to read chat transcripts with provable end to end encryption down to the CPU registers and TPM endorsement chain of the software involved. Provable privacy via remote attestation effectively gives you provable inference for free. reply ronfriedhaber 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Attestable does great work on the subject:\n https://attestable.com/blog/proving-llms-scale reply ygouzerh 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Are they doing that because they are scared that Moonshot might route queries to Claude? reply wcoenen 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > The goal of provable inference (PI) is to enable a user with limited computational resources and without any information about the weights to verify that a response came from the requested model Isn't this already solved by watermarking? This page said they would provide an API to check whether a given text has the watermark for a Claude model: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-text-watermark reply hobofan 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n That's two entirely different types of things that are being verified. Watermarking checks whether a _specific text_ came from a provider. PI checks that the text (that you already know came from the provider) was produced with _a specific set of weights_. PI is mainly useful for AI supply-chain security. reply seurimas 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Not to the degree needed for actually verifying the things this verifies. You could probably quite easily produce a non-Claude model that generates text that conforms to Claude watermarking. reply wcoenen 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n In the page I linked above, Anthropic explains that they use the techniques from SynthID-Text, where a secret watermarking key is involved. Only Anthropic knows this key, so only Anthropic can produce and detect the watermark. (But they also promised to provide an API through which other parties can check the watermark.) SynthID-Text paper: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08025-4 reply saifelse 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think there are two gaps with the watermark: - it only gives a high probability that the text was outputted from the model, whereas a crytographic proof is computationally infeasible to forge - it cannot be traced back to the input. You could \"wash\" the output from one model into another and it would be watermarked by the latter. With the crytographic proof, if you have the input, output, and \"commitment\", you can verify your exact input was processed by the model (or so the model provider says). reply dnnehgf 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n i guess the idea here is that if the end user cannot discriminate based on capability above some threshold, the premium that you can charge for more capable models either disappears entirely (if the end user is the principal source of truth on quality) or gets arbitraged down (if the end user is merely an agent of the principal source of truth on quality). to maintain that premium in perpetuity, you just need (a) trusted benchmarking services that can distinguish between premium and generic, and then (b) a way to prove to the end user that a given output is premium. so this solves for (b). reply jsrozner 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n \"The commitment to the model, and subsequent inference proofs, hide the weights of the model; hiding the architecture of the model is not a goal of this system.\" Your Claude is showing. reply ljlolel 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n already solved provable inference with encrypted chats on trustedrouter (using TEEs which most people don’t know about) reply arcwhite 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wager regulatory capture lies this way. Look for this becoming an in-hardware feature, with big fat patents on it, and then a push to demand all inference providers use \"TPM chipped\" hardware. reply jmort 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n What are the tradeoffs of an approach like this vs confidential computing primitives and attesting everything from untampered hardware all the way up to the served request? It seems more performant. Is it simply that they would have to reveal too much? reply FrasiertheLion 8 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n More so that attestation requires the model to be running on secure hardware, which is hard to do across all models that a company like Anthropic serves. In part because many of the accelerators they use may not support attestation, and even with ones that do, such as NVIDIA Blackwell, support is restricted to a single node (8 GPUs). Vera Rubin supports rack-scale encryption, but that is not broadly deployed yet and will not be the only accelerator they use to serve inference. reply anonymous908213 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > A user querying a model today cannot check that the responses came from the specific model that a developer claims is responding Indeed, I wonder what kind of developer would make users worry about that...? Funny PR stunt coming from a company that was in the news for silently routing ML-related requests to an inferior model. https://fortune.com/2026/06/10/anthropic-accu-claude-fable-5... > Unlike Fable’s other restrictions, such as around cybersecurity and biology, which openly redirect users to a less powerful model with a visible notification, the system card emphasized that this is “not visible to the user.” The model still responds, but uses “interventions to limit Claude’s effectiveness” without telling the user it’s doing so. reply with 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n isn't model substitution a vendor accountability thing? and token tampering, is that not infra security? do either of these need 70-95x the GPU time in \"zksnark\" proofs? reply ex-aws-dude 9 hours ago | prev [–] \n > Some proposals to regulate and pace AI development rely on monitoring hardware and how it is used. For example, a provider might be required to devote its hardware to inference rather than to training a powerful new model. The provider might try to cheat by serving cheaper models or using compute in an unexpected way, freeing up resources for training. How can a regulator reliably ensure that the hardware is used for inference? Is this even a real problem? Forcing a company to only do inference? Sounds like Anthropic's dream scenario reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
