  "text": " This Month in Ladybird – October 2026 | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login This Month in Ladybird – October 2026 ( ladybird.org ) 114 points by BitPirate 10 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 38 comments help \n \n quux 10 hours ago | next [–] \n Ladybird has been really shaping up over the last month or so, I pull and build the latest bits every morning and use it for low stakes web browsing (HN, news sites, reddit, youtube, blogs, etc.) and it's getting very close to the point where I forget that it's a pre-alpha. Very exciting to watch a completely new browser/browser engine come together. Reminds me of the early days of Mozilla and Gecko Posted with Ladybird :) reply sashank_1509 8 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I suspect in a year or so, an entire browser can be prompted by AI and then it won’t feel all that cool anymore. Unless AI hits a wall soon (unlikely). reply Wowfunhappy 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I've actually used AI to create a WebKit port (not an original browser). The amazing thing is that it worked--but it still took months of issue triage. Even when the AI was working on its own, it had to spend enormous amounts of time just on compiling and testing. There are so many moving pieces, and each individual issue could mean the AI spinning for days. Even if a future AI can drive web browser development completely autonomously, I suspect it will take a huge amount of real-world time because that's just the shape of the work no matter how \"smart\" you are. reply Omniusaspirer 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yep, I've been building a custom H2/H3 server for nearly 6 months now maxing my Claude 200 plan every week. Very pleased with the results but it's definitely not a quick or cheap task, it's constant decision fatigue and tons of rigorous testing on every piece in turn. Fuzzing, mutation testing, Miri, protocol conformance suites, H2load, actual networked runs, so on and so on. reply sashank_1509 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I mean it worked, if scaling doesn’t stop and it can do this non stop at GPT Ultra speeds, this whole task will be done in weeks, maybe lesser. Doesn’t refute my point, ladybird is unfortunately going to be a pointless project soon. Though I guess if you have fun, who cares. reply Wowfunhappy 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No, because the bottleneck isn't just model speed. The model has to literally compile the engine and run the massive test suites. Some of that could be sped up if you had a real build farm instead of my little computer, but there's a limit to how much you can parallelize things. And remember, I was porting WebKit, not remaking it, which would be orders of magnitude more work. So you're not going to get to \"build me an original web browser from scratch, make no mistakes\" and get a new app the next morning or even next week, I don't think. reply xydone 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Why is it that no conversation can be had about anything without AI being shoehorned into it, what did the parent comment even have to do with this reply JumpCrisscross 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n AI is trained on human works. The preferences Ladybird expresses will influence those browsers. reply arecsu 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n \"[...] as far as we know, no other browser can do this today. Here’s a text animation while JavaScript blocks the main thread in 1.5 second bursts, with Ladybird in the top left:\" This is amazing!! I'm really surprised no other browser is able to do this. Is there an implicit \"security\" issue with it, or maybe standardization when it comes to keeping some values from DOM-Javascript in sync? Or simply an oversight in their part? In any case, congratulations to Ladybird's team. Computers are so much more capable of raw performance and computing, and it's nice that some developers out there do care heavily about it reply nvme0n1p1 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Opera's old engine (Presto) was able to do this too, before Opera switched to Blink. This is a perfect example of why browser diversity is important. reply king_crimson 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n afaik the decision to run animations on or off the main thread is a recurring point of debate not just in web browsers.\nOn iOS for example SwiftUI runs them on the main thread while UIKit w/ CoreAnimation runs them off it. I don’t remember the specific reasons for these decisions but the point is, as with many things there are pros and cons to both approaches. reply CitrusFruits 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Ladybird is such an exciting project, really excited to try it out for day to day browsing when they get into Alpha. reply quux 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n It's very easy to build it yourself if you want to try it right now. https://github.com/LadybirdBrowser/ladybird/blob/master/Docu... reply anthk 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'd love a Flatpak... and maybe Gopher support in the browser ;) reply KingMob 12 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n I will stop using the web entirely before I use anything made by Charlie Kirk-praising, DHH-defending, white supremacist-retweeting Andreas Kling. reply mempko 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Honest question for people. I'm a C++ dev and I'm seeing a lot of moves to rust. Everyone is using agents to move stuff to rust and I tried it myself. The amount of insane hardrive space usage (over 90GB of build artifacts that constantly need cleanup) is that normal? What am I doing wrong. Are people seeing advantages after a move to rust that wasn't there in C++ (assuming no new features). Like is your project better after moving to rust in obvious ways? reply suriyaG 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Once I spent a month debugging a UAF bug, in a messy C++ codebase. With rust, those class of errors are very difficult to arrive at, unless you specifically make it so. with rust, the \"risk\" gets moved from runtime to compiletime. I'll take a 1TB build bloat over code crashing in prod. any day. reply reallyreason 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I have the same problem and am regularly `cargo clean`ing hundreds of gigabytes of build artifacts. It is a real problem. I feel like I need minimum 4TB of SSD space just to comfortably work at the scale I'm working now in Rust. That's just for the working space of the target folders across worktrees. I just think agents benefit a lot from having unavoidable feedback in the dev loop, and Rust affords that through its strictness. It's easier for agents to fall into the pit of success with Rust. I do think it's somewhat lacking in compile-time features, though. reply pornel 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's expected. Cargo has very conservative cache cleanup (once per month IIRC). There's incremental compilation cache that generates lots of churn (although Rust's compilation unit is a crate, \"queries\" in the compiler are cached individually, creating many cache entries per function per type). The other half of the disk space is debug info, because abstractions that optimize down to zero runtime code still carry all of their source-level debug info. These can be adjusted in build profiles if you're ok with coarser debug info or less fine-grained caching. reply afdbcreid 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Cargo has no cache cleanup for `target` on stable. reply nicoburns 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's normal. But you can somewhat mitigate by: - Making sure you compile with as few profiles as possible. - Disabling debug info (or setting it to \"line-table-only\") for the default profile. reply andoowhy 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's normal, but they're working on it: https://blog.rust-lang.org/inside-rust/2026/08/18/reducing-t... reply ahartmetz 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Shared libraries (i.e. not building every damn dependency) would fix this problem real quick in many situations, but of course, Rust people don't want to commit to an ABI in order to keep their options open. I don't think it makes sense much longer - at this point \"can't do shared libraries\" seems more like cultural inertia. Two or so ABI breaks with a few years between them also seem quite survivable. reply slopinthebag 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n 90gb? i've ran cargo clean on a single project and reclaimed over 700gb before. but i still love rust ofc reply ASalazarMX 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Jesus, that makes the JavaScript ecosystem look moderate by comparison! reply nicoburns 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The difference is that this accumulates over time. You'll only get this if you haven't run \"clean\" for some time (likely months). reply slopinthebag 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n it was days, but it was related to something the agent was doing with tests and targets, where it was basically compiling a billion variations of the almost 500,000loc, 1100 deps, and 50+ crate project it had slopped together lol. reply ranger_danger 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Isn't the advantage memory safety? When I mentioned, after C++26 failed its vote, that maybe they should use this extra time to focus more on memory safety, I got downvoted instead. Surely that attitude isn't helping their situation. reply jraph 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You probably read too much into the downvotes, and people who downvoted you in the other thread might not even be the same people deciding on the future of C++. I didn't downvote you (that's not something I do) but I didn't find your comment particularly relevant or on topic. This disapproval thing and working on memory safety don't seem related at all. Someone told you they had the impression that you were pushing for Rust without mentioning it. Your comment might have felt like you were backseat driving as well. More memory safety in C++ would be cool, but do you have actual ideas on how to achieve this? There are a lot of reasons people could have downvoted you. You shouldn't worry too much about it though, just relax and forget about it. It's fine. Happens to the best of us. reply anthk 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n >Flatpak It was about time. BTW, when this gem uses EGui instead of QT6 (nothing against QT, but once everything uses Rust, it will be no reason to keep it) it will shine. reply moron4hire 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Would love to see where all the folks from 5 years ago, breathlessly declaring it's futile to start a new browser project, are now and what they are doing with their lives. reply Rabbit504030201 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Because it IS futile. Occam's Razor shaped hobby project, that what it is. Where is the market share? Nowhere, because existing browsers are good enough and no corporation pushes Ladybird. Showing how far one can go with modern AI tooling does matter only a little when all you do is looking at Chromium/Firefox code and recompiling the logic of it in another language. I'll stick with Firefox. reply kvuj 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Showing how far one can go with modern AI tooling does matter only a little when all you do is looking at Chromium/Firefox code and recompiling the logic of it in another language. Is that what Ladybird does? Can you point to a real world example? From the blog post, it seems there are real world differences (look at the performance when resizing with heavy JS!). You're also mixing arguments. The original arguments against ladybird (which I vividly remember as I loved following them back then) were that it would be impossible to arrive at something that works for all use cases. Ladybird would be able to render toy websites like hacker news, but nothing of worth. It seems they were proven evidently wrong. reply JumpCrisscross 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Where is the market share? Where was the market share for browsers in the 1980s? reply jljljl 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n But that's the thing: it's not 1980 anymore, browsers exist, and they have marketshare reply JumpCrisscross 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > it's not 1980 anymore, browsers exist, and they have marketshare Okay, sure. What was the market share for Chrome in 1999? There are legitimate arguments against Ladybird. But pointing to the market share of a non-existent product, in a category on the verge of a paradigm shift from AI, is not one of them. reply anthk 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Waterfox switched their AD blocking engine to Rust. It's several times faster than UBo. reply iamnothere 7 hours ago | prev [–] \n Wow, they now beat Safari in tests passed. Catching up to Firefox too. Lots of momentum here. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
 "readme:bitcoinjs/bitcoinjs-lib": {
