es to much worse places than gendering the machine. reply mikestylz 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Should we assume that all software is more compromised than the kernel? Absolutely, the front door has always been unlocked. We were previously protected by the economics of exploit detection favoring the developers, but now we have entered a new world. The issues arise almost everywhere we look. It's frustrating because the user experience in edge systems has barely changed in the last fifteen years. We could have rode the advances in semiconductor manufacturing to embed more layers of protection such as CHERI while slightly slowing down the rate of performance improvements. Instead we have only marginally upgraded security and largely added features which the users scarcely notice. I am hopeful about the long term prospects of formal verification, but having humans reviewing the proof statements and confirming they match our desired properties will take many years. Meanwhile, AI assisted hacking is an urgent issue. So we will have to delegate implementation, proof specification, and proof development to the AIs in the short term. Maybe that goes well, but who knows. Interesting times. reply asveikau 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n For all of my time being aware of this industry, it has generally been understood that privilege escalation bugs are common. This has always been a thing. A lot of people say, commonly and for many years, that if your security depends on there not being privilege escalation bugs, you don't have security. reply chrismarlow9 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This. Privilege escalation locally is almost a given in most cases. reply bombcar 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The dirty secret is multi-user died in the 90s and privilege escalation has basically been considered table-stakes since then. reply tptacek 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think this is basically true, but it didn't \"die\"