nt that we've found all the holes at a given analysis capability level -- if something a hundred times as capable looks, it will likely find many more. That's how it will be. reply Muromec 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n >Is the kernel simply too complex to reason about? Is it an underlying flaw in the security model or language implementation? There is nothing magical about kernel. Look at the code anywhere and it has bugs, lots of them. Security bugs are just bugs. More often than not they are silently there and don't prevent you from using the software and you can't react and fix them because of that. Give it time and they accumulate reply dingaling911 11 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Have you written a kernel? Even toy kernels are hard to make without vulnerabilities. reply sreekanth850 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Human brain mostly take the shortest available path for a solution to reduce the cognitive load. When a system complexity increases probability of cutting the corners will get increased. This is a known pattern. Models just do the polar opposite. They try to cover almost every hypothetical edge cases. They never get tired or bored, nor do they suffer from glucose limits, mental fatigue, or shifting attention spans, which is one cause of over engineering, but valuable in vulnerability tracing. reply NooneAtAll5 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > How did we get to the point where there are a thousand privilege escalation exploits in the kernel? it's been rule of thumb basically forever that there is 1 bug per ~10KLOC for any project linux kernel is millions of lines, so thousands of bugs being hidden there is about on point (tho obviously far smaller serious escalation ones, obviously, but you're exaggerating as well) reply jiggawatts 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > 1 bug per ~10KLOC for any project 1 bug per 10 lines -- not kilolines -- is much closer to the actual \"bug rate\". You may be thinking \"1 serious security vulnerability bug\", but in my experience that too is much closer to 1 per 100 lines to 1 per 1,000 lines, depending on the type of application. reply p-e-w 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n How can it be surprising that a software made from 40+ million lines of memory-unsafe code has hundreds of critical bugs? reply wahern 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n More than half of those lines are from drivers, drivers which wouldn't be relevant to a VM environment. Core kernel code for any particular architecture is less than 10 million, but some of that is filesystems or subsystems not in use or available to an unprivileged user on a typical server, so possibly maybe 5 million or less. And the article says they only targeted 2.8 million SLoC. reply hoppp 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n There are always more left. reply ChickeNES 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n And memory safety doesn't prevent logic errors (or using 4 as a private key for that matter) reply pixl97 21 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This said memory unsafe code just likely has more bugs because of all the footguns it has on top of all the other security work you need to do with it. reply 0xbadcafebee 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Funny how OpenBSD is \"memory-unsafe\" yet doesn't have these issues. Could it be that the programmer might have something to do with it? reply icepush 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n As an OpenBSD lover and frequent user I will tell you it does not support but a significant fraction of the hardware Linux does, and has many features that are reduced in functionality or just gone compared to Linux. Is it bulletproof? Definitely! But you need the genius of Albert Einstein as well as the patience of Mother Theresa to actually use it in production. reply Muromec 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Can't have a bug in a feature you never implemented and can't have another arising from unexpected interaction of two features you didn't implement. It's the one security practice that actuuuualy works reply wahern 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n For most server setups OpenBSD's hardware support is adequate, and this is especially true for VMs. It's laptops, peripherals, and bleeding edge, high-performance or specialized devices where driver support is a PITA. But that's not something I've encountered running my own private servers. It would be nice to have something like ZFS, but mostly that's a peace of mind and convenience thing--would be nice to be able to stream backups somewhere rather than push around dumps and tarballs, though I have a script for that. reply cyberax 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Is anyone spending millions of dollars on checking OpenBSD? But it also got its share of use-after-free and OOB access vulnerabilities this year: https://feedly.com/cve/vendors/openbsd reply 0xbadcafebee 27 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The orders of magnitude fewer security vulns for OpenBSD are not due to less testing. Everybody has always known OpenBSD is more secure, and it's not from using a different language. The reason is the code. Linux was not designed to be secure. OpenBSD was. It's that simple. reply esseph 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Funny how OpenBSD is \"memory-unsafe\" yet doesn't have these issues. First of all... Do you really believe that to be true? reply gre 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Software really is that complicated. reply 0c3ca83 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The Linux kernel is simply not good quality code. AI helps find vulnerabilities, but the way things are written were already fertile ground. reply est31 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The kernel has the problem that basically every bug in it is also a security vulnerability. All software has bugs. Had Linux been written in Rust, and I strongly recommend a rewrite, it would still have bugs, and thus would still have vulnerabilities. But they'd be way less. reply anonymous908213 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > and I strongly recommend a rewrite, Oh, you strongly recommend rewriting tens of millions of lines of code? Why don't you do it yourself? Just a prompt away, right? reply est31 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Just a prompt away, right? Humans manually typing out the bytes of source code has been an outdated method, since around a year. It simply doesn't scale as much as those same humans directing agentic teams. So yes, it would be about prompts. The hard part isn't the technology for the language transition, that one is present today. pgrust shows the way. It's ensuring maintainers are cooperative, i.e. a social problem. Eventually you diverge too much if you do a fork, so it's better done in cooperation with upstream. Just check how long it took LLVM or GCC to switch off svn for example, and a language switch is much more involved than a VCS switch. reply anonymous908213 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > pgrust shows the way. You mean the pgrust which was recently caught for cheating benchmarks and incapable of even performing basic queries outside the benchmark set? Would love to see pgrust debunked by Jepsen like this other AI-generated, written in Rust, \"formally verified\" scam. https://jepsen.io/analyses/particledb-1.1.4 reply est31 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think the pgrust controversy should be resolved now? https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50209450 reply anonymous908213 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I don't consider a cheater downplaying their cheating to resolve the fact that they cheated, no. Every cheater has a defense when caught, and theirs was not convincing. At this point I think the only thing that could redeem their brand would be paying for a Jepsen analysis to verify pgrust is as good as they claim it is; I have zero reason to trust the words from the horse's mouth. reply Muromec 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Kernel will eventually move to rust, but not the dramatic and fast way reply shoobiedoo 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Won't AI's ability to discover these bugs reduce the need to rewrite things in rust? If the major benefit to rewriting them is memory safety, I mean... reply Gigachad 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n That's assuming the current models are able to find all the bugs, and that we won't have a better model out every few months that will be able to find even more obscure bugs. We would also have to burn a trillion tokens a day verifying all the C code the hard way forever going forward. It would be cheaper to rewrite the kernel in Rust, subsystem and driver by driver at a time. reply shoobiedoo 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I see. Fair enough reply est31 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n That's a fair question, and actually one I ask myself too. AI doesn't give a strong guarantee that things work though, while Rust gives a much stronger (not absolute) guarantee. But maybe the way of the future is to auto-generate proofs that the code is memory safe and then just leave things where they are, instead of doing rewrites that require maintainer buy ins. The advantage of C plus proofs would be that the AI generated proofs could live separately so it wouldn't touch workflows of the maintainers. The only required output they have to process is evidence of vulnerabilities in changes proposed to them. They could outsource even that to contributors. reply jkelleyrtp 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n fwiw at work we've been looking at what the options are more memory-safe kernels out there. Not many options. If there were a \"safe linux\" maintained by like canonical or redhat or whatever we'd use it. For datacenter workloads like sandboxes and virtualization I think it'd do well. reply ventedmouse 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n “Claude rewrite this project, its 40 million LOC, make no mistake” reply AnimalMuppet 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I recall reading that every IBM mainframe OS release had 1000 bugs in it. (This was in the 1970s/80s.) Sorry I can't supply a source, after 4-5 decades. It was probably an article in Datamation magazine. Now, I don't know how many of those bugs were privilege escalations, or could be leveraged to be. I'm not sure that people tried to exploit in the same way back then (or, if they did, if they said anything publicly about the results). Still, even if the majority of those bugs were not exploitable, OSes were much smaller then. So that's my conclusion of how we got here: We've always been here. reply flomo 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Mainframes went right to virtual machines, so possibly bypassed much of the multiuser faux-security which has always plagued unix. Of course, now they run Linux partitions and get all the same bugs. reply esseph 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n The surrounding systems that connect to mainframe also expect and support those bugs. For 40+ yr now :) reply antonvs 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > How did we get to the point where there are a thousand privilege escalation exploits in the kernel? We developed AIs that can find them. reply spartanatreyu 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > How did we get to the point where there are a thousand privilege escalation exploits in the kernel? > > We developed AIs that can find them. We could already find them before AI. We already knew they were there, and we already knew how to prevent them in the first place. See previous discussion: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49705794 reply antonvs 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Why do you think we didn’t find these 948 vulnerabilities before now? reply esseph 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > We could already find them before AI. Not at this speed and cost. reply spartanatreyu 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Not at this speed and cost. I'd argue that the resources (i.e. time, money and effort) needed to create the tooling that would have prevented all of these issues would have been orders of magnitude less than the resources that have gone into \"AI\". reply azan_ 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It’s a bad argument. AI isn’t single use. Current iteration of AI is already there and serving it is cheap. So you get great tool for detecting these issues in the future at low cost. Without AI the cost wouldn’t really decrease with time. And also it costs me 200$ now to find these bugs. I don’t think I could hire software engineer for that much. reply perching_aix 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n amazing things happen when you decide that device drivers should be a part of your kernel codebase reply dangelosaurus 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n This is my team’s work. I’m happy to try to answer questions or take questions back to the team. And we're hiring! https://openai.com/careers/search/?q=cyber reply konaraddi 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n As models get more advanced and generate an increasing throughput of increasingly higher quality results, how are you determining when to trust its results and when human review is needed on what? E.g., 948 results is a high number to review in a timely manner unless your team is very large or spending several months combing through the results EDIT: nvm i shouldve kept reading, answered > At this scale, manually investigating every report is impractical for our research team.\nWe therefore focused on validating findings that a) were reachable by a low-privileged user, b) required no special capabilities, and c) produced measurable evidence of memory corruption. reply BoppreH 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's a bittersweet result, like finding asbestos in your house. I'd rather know than not, but I would much prefer not having it in the first place. I understand this work was based on a dated snapshot. Will there be any continuous effort, scanning new PRs as they land, to prevent new weaknesses from being added? reply ky1ebot1337 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n (I am the author the pipeline, just created an account to answer some questions :D)\nMy goal is to work towards a vuln-free Internet. What I observed is that stronger models can find many more vulnerabilities that weaker models cannot. If compute permits (there are other projects to secure besides Linux), I plan to scan Linux again with every newer generation of cyber models. reply Lerc 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Have you given thought on how you can use this info to estimate how many vulnerabilities there are? It will be difficult to claim that the problem has been solved without an idea of how big the problem is. Given that vulnerabilities are almost entirely unknown, you are inherently trying to measure the undetected. Is there a way to hypothesize how many vulnerabilities there are and them make a prediction on how many of those will be found by a model to get a sense of how accurate the estimate is? reply ahsillyme 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Thanks. I'm not a programmer so I can't understand what is happening in the kernel by looking at the changelog, so I'll ask: Are the problems remediated structurally so that nothing like it can occur in the future, rather than just patching it up? Are there \"lessons learned\" that are being applied and documented here (like, \"this problem looks like it's shape transports to other subsystems as well, let's fix them all in a disciplined way\")? reply esseph 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I can't comment specifically on the Linux kernel piece, but I want to point out, broadly, that security has generally been an afterthought, and very little gets invested into it in terms of time and money - as a general rule. Multiply that behavior times the number of commercial and mobile and b2b apps over 50+ years, and that's where we are. There will likely be many more leaks, and hacks, and outages. And then in trying to be proactive to fix old systems, that will cause new proactive outages, and then eventually many things will be better. But, it's going to take a lot of time and investment, and a lot of (majority) of currently unsupported devices will have to be complete replaced / scrapped / ewaste. On the scale of Nation-State initiatives. This is across all operating systems, firmware, software and devices of all kinds, etc. LLMs to security are like a bomb went off. We still have to wait for things to fall over before the dust will even clear enough to see what we have to work with. reply 0x00cl 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I'm going to assume that there was \"old\" code that caused LPE vulnerabilities, but, The Linux Foundation have their own project Sashiko ( https://sashiko.dev/ ), which analyzes each commit to the Linux Kernel for bugs and vulnerabilities using Googles models, my question is then, how many of the vulnerabilities you found were also caught by Sashiko? reply paxy 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If Sashiko had found them then they wouldn't have been merged, so my guess is 0 reply emmelaich 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I'd like to see a break down by age of code and part of kernel (drivers, networking, core ,etc ..) reply basilikum 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The high number is really impression to the point of making me skeptic. Are these distinct vulnerabilities or are a lot of the related in the way vulns often are? The article says \"The target virtual machines ran a stock desktop operating system\". Are the vulns in the kernel specifically or does this include software running as root? reply ky1ebot1337 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n These are distinct vulnerabilities counted by the number of patches. The patch generation system performs variant analysis. So, each patch can potentially stop multiple exploits. The raw exploits number is higher than what we reported in the post. We don't want to cause the confusion why #patch != #exploit, so we used the smaller number there.\nThese vulns are all kernel specific. Userspace software running as root was not in scope of the analysis. reply farisght 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I wish these blog posts included statistics on the categories of deficiencies that cause these vulnerabilities, and proposed measures to avoid them A system that admits THOUSANDS of such vulnerabilities is certainly flawed in several fundamental aspects reply jethro_tell 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It sounds like they can’t go through them all enough to have that data yet. reply esseph 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Kernel is far too big to throw at a single LLM, just like a human. It is.. I think somebody said 40mil lines of code. So you have to chunk it down to workable pieces, and then if you collect enough data of that process you can look at higher level stats. Same for every other existing piece of software, btw. reply rmbryan 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n With these vulnerabilities and the ones not listed that are sure to exist, how is the internet still up? How are my lights on? Why aren't power plants exploding by the dozens? What are the mitigating factors that prevent this level of vulnerability from being an existential threat to humanity? reply tantalor 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n In general computer systems have been known unreliable since the 1980s, especially when networked. The entire cyber security industry operates not by preventing intrusions in the first place, but by rapidly mitigating them when they inevitably happen. Hopefully this type of work to automatically discover & patch vulnerability leads to much more reliable software, and the remove the need for cyber security. reply rmbryan 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Okay, thank you. That's a helpful reframing. reply PinkMilkshake 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Just in the last month multiple governments had to shut down important online services due to a PHP vulnerability, and seven EU countries have had disruption to their food supply after millions of dollars' worth of it was cooked in hacked fridges. We aren't at existential yet, but we seem to be getting there. reply nomel 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The governments buy them up [1]. Nukes wouldn't be required for WW3. [1] https://www.cyberdefensemagazine.com/zero-day-market-the-gov... reply rmbryan 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n article from 2013 reply nomel 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n 2022: https://www.darkreading.com/cyberattacks-data-breaches/googl... 2026: https://medium.com/@hammaadmughal/the-invisible-bazaar-insid... etc. Concept of governments being main buyers doesn't change by year. reply paxy 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Because critical systems are mostly not accessible from the open internet reply Veserv 38 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Cybersecurity operates under the \"not enough bears\" theory of security. There are a million people in a valley and a male and female bear. You do not need to outrun the bears, you just need to outrun the two slowest people. Next year there are more bears, but you still only need to outrun a few people. Then, one day, there are a million bears and they eat you all. But that day has not happened yet. To explain more seriously, almost all visible cyberattacks are carried out by profit-motivated criminals. This has a very clear cost-benefit analysis; your revenue is hacking organizations and extorting them for available money, and your costs are developing and deploying attacks. The ROI is outrageously good easily being on the order of 10x to 100x. If it is so easy and so profitable why do not they just hack all of the millions of organizations on the planet with millions of dollars of available cash for trillions of dollars of revenue? Sure, all they have to do is create a trillion dollar revenue commercial organization bootstrapping themselves with no outside investors, loans, or access to financial resources or the financial system. I mean, it took Mark Zuckerberg 20 years and a metric ton of VC investment and public investment to make 200 billion dollars in revenue. So what kind of loser can not make a commercial organization 5x larger with a trillion dollars in revenue in less time with no outside investors? Are they even trying? Just make more revenue than any other commercial organization in the entire world and extract that money in a freely usable form, how hard can that be? They are not there yet, but they are trying. If you look at cybercrime reports you see something like 3x-10x growth year over year. Ransomware reports from a few years ago were showing a 3x year over year growth in number of attacks and simultaneously a 3x year over year growth in ransom amount with the averages being on the order of 100,000 dollars. Chump change for any serious organization, but give that a few years of 3x and that starts to look like real money. However, as it becomes real money it becomes harder and harder to launder it into a usable form. Sure, you could hit someone for 30 million dollars, but how are you going to get that into your bank account? Cryptocurrency helps some, but even there the big money tries to steer away from large overt crime. Maybe you could launder a few tens of billions a year that way, but that is still a far cry from the trillions for everything everywhere. And of course even assuming you can scale your organization 10x year over year it still takes a decade of sustained growth, scaling your hiring and organization across 9 orders of magnitude, to get to billions per year. A literal decade even if you were growing at a outrageous pace for a outrageous amount of time just to reach a fraction of the total addressable market. A decade of that and you are still nowhere near blowing up all the power plants. Give it time, we need a lot of bears to get there. reply nas 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I've always considered local escalations to be possible in Linux (i.e. having user access almost certainly means you can get root). However, 948 vulnerabilities is kind of shocking. Hopefully we are getting to the end of the part where it \"gets worse before it gets better\". reply bzmrgonz 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder what Sidero Labs has to say about this article. My hopes are pegged on their efforts. I have my wagon ready to hitch on their horses. They are not a one pony show anymore guys, Talos OS can be standalone server OS, a hypervisor server or cluster, and their flagship of k8s node or cluster. We get all that in an immutable gitops ready OS. I'm practically sold on their offering. reply rishabhd 53 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n Curious question - if any organization sponsored a 1.2mn USD program to find vulnerabilities in Linux, will they find these many vulnerabilities? Or use a professional software like Checkmarx or Veracode to do it with 1.2mn USD in license + human effort. Is it a funding + will problem or is it an AI problem? reply IcyWindows 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I think this is one area where Windows has an advantage. ACLs, Appcontainers with capabilities, etc have been something in the OS for more than a decade. reply viraptor 22 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n Linux has effectively same options and more via LSM. I'm not sure why ACL is mentioned here since it exists on Linux too. What advantage do you mean specifically? reply jasonjayr 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There are a lot of LPE's in Windows too. Remember that it started out as a single user operating system, it took a long time to get software on board to not always running as admin. reply easton 51 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Was NT ever single user? I thought they built it with network/multiuser in mind. (I agree that maybe applications could’ve been built for 3.1/9x and expect a single user, but the os expects multiple). reply perching_aix 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Considering the amount of crazy shit even just user access allows you to do, by design, an LPE is not even really necessary. reply getcrunk 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > For the near and medium term, having access to a low-privileged account on an operating system, that isn’t up-to-date on its latest available patches should be considered functionally equivalent to root access for a well-resourced threat actor. *buries head in sand* *cries* reply kccqzy 3 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n LPEs are frequent enough that people no longer use low-privilege user accounts to sandbox potentially malicious software. They are only used to control access for well-behaved users without any intent to subvert the system, not attackers. The difference here reminds me of the distinction between the regular Paxos algorithm and the Byzantine Paxos algorithm. reply rubinlinux 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This has been true for DECADES. reply basilikum 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Regular LPEs are not novel. The frequency has just increased a lot. reply bakugo 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Some distros even include LPE as a feature ( cough Ubuntu Server) reply zahlman 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > We've developed an autonomous vulnerability discovery, exploitation, and patching pipeline. In one run with our cyber model, it validated and generated patches for 948 local privilege escalation vulnerabilities in Linux at an API cost of $1.2 million. Imagine if open-source organizations other than the Linux Foundation (and Mozilla I guess) could throw $1.2M of API-rate LLM usage at security. reply komali2 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n This, but for anything Capital-gated. Imagine if all universities could get access to radiotelescope time, atom smasher time, quantum compute research lab access... frontier models on massive datacenters represents a huge new category of captured commons. reply ksec 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder if they could run it on OpenBSD and FreeBSD as well. reply tiffanyh 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Anthropic ran Mythos against OpenBSD and only had 1 reported vulnerability. https://www.anthropic.com/glasswing reply hoppp 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Well at least they are doing public service while hacking the planet. reply clvx 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n and yet thousands of iot devices will not get these updates. This is gonna be fun. reply tarpitt 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Oh no, they will not be secured against their own users :( reply tamimio 4 hours ago | prev [–] \n These are becoming more and more marketing materials than actual findings, because OAI probably hit the wall in making their models smarter (just like how google try to specialize their models in legal or whatever), but they need to remain relevant in the news. Sol 6.1 sucked so bad in reasoning that either it’s actually a bad model or users behind the scenes get degraded outcome while using all the resources for these marketing stunts. Stop, it’s embarrassing, make better models or be gone, at least we will get cheaper RAM to advance the development of open weights models. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
