source center Blog Events & webinars Community Forum Customer Stories Integrations Support Customer support Training GitHub Status Company About Partners Changelog GitHub Universe GitHub Universe The event for developers, by developers — talks, workshops, and what’s next for GitHub. Pricing Search / Sign in Sign up Appearance settings \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n\n \n Dismiss alert \n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n {{ message }} \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n gpustack gpustack Repository navigation Code Issues 661  ( 661 ) Pull requests 81  ( 81 ) Discussions Actions Projects Wiki Security and quality 11  ( 11 ) Insights More items \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n gpustack \n Security \n Advisories \n GHSA-gj9m-f8ph-pmhr \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Authentication bypass via trusted loopback source (reverse-proxy TCP peer or X-GPUStack-Real-IP header) grants unauthenticated admin\n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n Critical\n \n \n\n \n gitlawr \n published\n GHSA-gj9m-f8ph-pmhr \n Jul 31, 2026 \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Package \n\n \n \n \n\n \n gpustack \n ( pip )\n \n \n \n\n \n Affected versions \n\n < 2.2.0 \n \n\n \n Patched versions \n\n 2.2.0 \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n Description \n \n\n \n \n Impact \n In GPUStack before v2.2.0, get_current_user (in gpustack/api/auth.py ) \npromotes any request whose source is considered loopback to the built-in \nadministrator account when force_auth_localhost is False (the default). \nThe \"source\" is derived by client_ip_getter , which can be made to report \n 127.0.0.1 without any credentials in two ways: \n \n Same-host reverse proxy (TCP peer). In the canonical self-hosted shape — \nGPUStack behind a same-host reverse proxy with proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:<port> — every remote request arrives at GPUStack with TCP \npeer 127.0.0.1 , so the loopback auto-admin branch fires. No header or \ncredential is required. Verified end-to-end against HEAD 6bee5883 . \n X-GPUStack-Real-IP header. A forged X-GPUStack-Real-IP: 127.0.0.1 \nheader can cause the request source to be treated as loopback. \n \n Either path yields full, unauthenticated administrative access over the \nnetwork: enumerate and modify users, models, workers, and configuration; mint \npersistent admin API keys (scope [\"*\"] , valid from any source IP thereafter); \nread worker credentials and cloud/provider secrets. Combined with admin-only \nfunctionality such as POST /v2/inference-backends/from-yaml , this leads to \ncode execution on worker hosts. \n This vulnerability has been observed exploited in the wild against a v2.1.1 \ndeployment (attacker-created custom backends running reverse shells). \n Root cause \n Trusting the network source of a request (socket peer, or a client-controllable \nheader) as an authentication signal. When GPUStack is fronted by a same-host \nreverse proxy, the socket peer is always loopback, so the \"localhost is trusted\" \nassumption no longer holds. \n Patches \n Fixed in gpustack 2.2.0 : the loopback auto-admin path was removed and \nclient-IP / forwarding-header handling was hardened. Upgrade to 2.2.0 or later. \n Workarounds \n \n Set force_auth_localhost=true to require authentication even for loopback \nrequests. \n Ensure the reverse proxy / gateway cannot cause GPUStack to treat external \ntraffic as loopback, and strip any client-supplied X-GPUStack-Real-IP \nheader at the edge. \n Keep the API off untrusted networks (VPN / edge authentication). \n \n Remediation (in the fix) \n Do not derive authentication from the request's network source. Remove implicit \nloopback-based admin, and only accept a real-client-IP header from an \nauthenticated/trusted gateway (never from arbitrary clients). \n Credit \n Independently reported by Nikko Enggaliano; Wei Yunzhou; and Rui Yang & Haoyu \nWang (Johns Hopkins University). Active in-the-wild exploitation was reported by \nJavier Sixto (ulex.fr). \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n Critical\n \n\n \n \n \n 9.8 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Network \n \n Attack complexity\n Low \n \n Privileges required\n None \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Unchanged \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n High \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n CVE-2026-102895\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-287 \n \n \n \n Improper Authentication \n When an actor claims to have a given identity, the product does not prove or insufficiently proves that the claim is correct.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-290 \n \n \n \n Authentication Bypass by Spoofing \n This attack-focused weakness is caused by incorrectly implemented authentication schemes that are subject to spoofing attacks.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-346 \n \n \n \n Origin Validation Error \n The product does not properly verify that the source of data or communication is valid.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n Credits \n\n \n \n \n \n \n nikkoenggaliano \n\n \n Finder \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n Shadowoyz \n\n \n Finder \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n You can’t perform that action at this time.\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n"
