runs in RISC-V machine mode (M-mode) with full address-space access, a caller could supply a stack pointer into TEE-exclusive memory and cause the register frame to be written there, making this an arbitrary M-mode write. The same unvalidated stack pointer pattern was present on both the user ecall entry path and the interrupt entry path. \n Impact \n An unprivileged REE application can direct the ESP-TEE trap entry to write attacker-influenced content into TEE-resident memory, breaking the REE/TEE isolation boundary. When the write targets TEE control-flow data that the TEE later dereferences, this can be escalated to arbitrary code execution within the TEE, effectively elevating the attacker from the REE to the TEE. Other outcomes include TEE memory corruption, denial of service, and system crashes. The attacker only needs local code execution in the REE; no TEE privileges are required. \n This issue affects Espressif SoCs with ESP-TEE support (ESP32-C5, ESP32-C6, ESP32-C61, ESP32-H2). \n Resolution \n The ESP-TEE trap entry now validates the REE-supplied stack pointer at the privilege-switch boundary before any register context is saved through it. A stack pointer whose save frame would overlap TEE memory is rejected before being dereferenced, terminating the call with a trusted-stack panic. The fix covers both the user ecall entry path and the interrupt entry path. \n Fix Commit Records \n \n \n \n Branch \n Commits \n \n \n \n \n master \n acb7d99 \n \n \n release/v6.1 \n 773761a \n \n \n release/v6.0 \n b85a7b0 \n \n \n release/v5.5 \n dabd37f \n \n \n \n Workarounds \n There is no runtime workaround for this issue. The fix is included in ESP-IDF v5.5.6 and will be included in v6.1.1 and v6.0.4 (yet to be released). Users are kindly advised to either update to a release that carries the fix or to cherry-pick the commit listed in the Fix Commit Records table above onto their downstream branch. \n Credits \n We would like to thank Xiaobye (@xiaobye_tw) of DEVCORE Research Team for reporting this vulnerability and following up on responsible disclosure. \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n Critical\n \n\n \n \n \n 9.3 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Local \n \n Attack complexity\n Low \n \n Privileges required\n None \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Changed \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n High \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n CVE-2026-113780\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-787 \n \n \n \n Out-of-bounds Write \n The product writes data past the end, or before the beginning, of the intended buffer.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-822 \n \n \n \n Untrusted Pointer Dereference \n The product obtains a value from an untrusted source, converts this value to a pointer, and dereferences the resulting pointer.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n Credits \n\n \n \n \n \n \n xiaobye-ctf \n\n \n Reporter \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n You can’t perform that action at this time.\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n"
